// R16 residual: a nested field whose sibling was moved. `p.inner.a` is
// moved into `moved`, and `fieldWasMoved` used to skip the whole `inner`
// field, so `p.inner.b` leaked. `p.tag` was already released.
//
// CLOSED 2026-09-16: `emitPartialRecordDrop` recurses. A path equal to
// `inner` still skips the field whole; a proper `inner.` prefix partial-
// drops the inner struct (skip `inner.a`, free `inner.b`). Measured 1000
// before on both witnesses (`leaks` 1000, counter ALLOC=3000 FREE=2000
// LIVE=1000) through this same host and counter on the tree before the
// change. AFTER: 0. It stays in the gate BECAUSE it is closed: pinned at
// 0, any nonzero reading re-opens it.
//
// What this fixture does NOT cover: a field written again after its move
// (R16 revival at field granularity). A field moved on only one branch
// of an `if` closed the same day (`branch_field.cell`).
//
// Status: parses, passes `cell check`. LLVM and MLIR refuse it (`String`
// crossing the C boundary is scalar-first-refused there); lives under
// examples/leaks/ so it is invisible to the top-level corpus, agreement and
// mlir-lowering loops (see examples/leaks/README.md).

pub struct Inner {
    owned a: String
    owned b: String
}

pub struct Outer {
    owned inner: Inner
    owned tag: String
}

pub fn print_int(copy value: Int);

pub fn main() {
    var i = 0
    while i < 1000 {
        let owned p: Outer = Outer { inner: Inner { a: "aaaaaaaa", b: "bbbbbbbb" }, tag: "tttttttt" }
        let owned moved: String = p.inner.a
        i = i + 1
    }
    print_int(i)
}
